      "title": "Pixelwise&InvertMask",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Pixelwise combines the masks including their alpha values, and InvertMask then inverts the area.\n\nThis processing is necessary because Attention Couple will throw an error if even a single pixel in the image remains unmasked.\n\nWhen adding more masks, duplicate the Pixelwise node, combine all masks together, and connect them to InvertMask.\n```mermaid\nMask1 ──┐\n                       \nMask2 ── [Pixelwise] ─► [Pixelwise] ─► [ InvertMask ]\n \nMask3 ────────────┘"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 529,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        580,
        1245
      ],
      "size": [
        340,
        435
      ],
      "flags": {
        "collapsed": false
      },
      "order": 6,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "Image to Image (i2i)",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "The images generated by Attention Couple can sometimes result in ambiguous interpretation or fused features when mask areas overlap. This group is used to correct such issues.\n\nIt is normally bypassed as a whole group, but disabling the bypass switches it to i2i (img2img) mode.\n\n- If you have the custom node **rgthree-comfy** installed, you can toggle the bypass of the entire group with a single button.\n\n# Tips\n- Loading the same image into \"Load Image (as Mask)\" makes it easier to see which areas are being masked.\n- Using Windows Paint or similar software to paint over fused areas with your ideal colors helps the AI understand your intention better.\n- The **Denoise** value in KSampler (Efficient) determines how closely the original image is followed.\n  Recommended ranges:\n  - **0.60～** : When you need to redraw clothing wrinkles, colors, etc.\n  - **0.40～0.59** : For light corrections such as fingers or shapes.\n  - **～0.30** : For minor fixes or during hires upscaling.\n- If you want to keep the composition mostly unchanged, the best method is to use image editing software that supports layers: overlay the image and erase the unwanted fused areas with an eraser tool.\n- For hires upscaling, first enlarge the image by n times using external software, then apply img2img with a low Denoise value.\n- Changes in contrast and color tones are almost unavoidable."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
